= Abstract

The Artemis learning management system provides a versatile quiz module supporting multiple-choice, drag-and-drop, and short-answer questions. Currently, the sub-components of these questions are stored as independent relational entities. This fragmented architecture necessitates expensive database joins, complex persistence operations, and results in a tightly coupled domain model. Furthermore, the module relies on manually maintained Application Programming Interface (API) contracts, leading to error-prone synchronization between the server and the web client.

This thesis proposes a comprehensive architectural refactoring of the quiz module, addressing these bottlenecks. First, this thesis redesigns concrete quiz questions as aggregate roots, embedding their exclusively owned components directly into JavaScript Object Notation (JSON) columns. This multi-model approach aims to significantly improve database query efficiency and simplify update operations while preserving historical data integrity. Second, the integration includes the module into the Artemis OpenAPI workflow, automatically generating robust TypeScript-Angular clients from machine-readable contracts. Finally, the quantitative evaluation assesses the refactored architecture regarding its impact on database performance, API consistency, and overall system maintainability.